@@ -1425,7 +1425,7 @@ applies when the event grows, so a tag can still be deleted after an operator lo
 
 *Concurrency bounds.* A rate limit is not a concurrency limit: the rows of ONE request cost at most 1 MiB of fan-out (content + 128 per row; the targets beyond that are dropped silently like an unreachable one, the first target is always kept), so a 64 KiB content reaches about 16 devices (15 when the content fills the whole body); at most 4 sends per user and 6 per node are in flight (`Nebu.Matrix.ToDeviceSendGate`, settings `to_device_max_concurrent_sends_per_user` and `to_device_max_concurrent_sends_per_node`; monitor based slots freed on `:DOWN`, node-local like the rate buckets), a send that finds no slot is answered 429 before its body is read (6 per node: each running send holds a pooled connection for its statements, so the slots stay well below the pool). Every statement of a send and of a delivery runs under `SET LOCAL statement_timeout` of 2 s (over it: 429 for a send, nothing stored and the txn id not burnt; an empty `to_device` for a sync).
 
-*Concurrency.* A request never waits for a lock while holding a pooled connection: the send takes the advisory lock `to_device_erasure|<user>` of the sender SHARED as a try-lock (the GDPR erasure takes that key exclusively; it is its OWN key, so a recipient's account data, cross-signing or key backup writer that holds `sync_global|<user>` never makes a message skip), the transaction has a short `lock_timeout`, a conflict is a 429; only the recipients that passed the shared-room join are locked (never one lock per requested id); the eviction, the acknowledgement and the stamp take `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` (a row another request holds is skipped, never waited for), and a delivery error (a lock, a statement timeout) never fails `/sync`: the answer is 200 with `to_device: {"events": []}`, the position is kept (nothing acknowledged or lost), the log names the kind only; a send takes `FOR KEY SHARE` on the target `devices` rows and a device delete locks the device row first, so a send racing a device delete never leaves rows for a recreated device of the same id; a send is a constant number of set-based statements however many recipients it names.
+*Concurrency.* A request never waits for a lock while holding a pooled connection: the send takes the advisory lock `to_device_erasure|<user>` of the sender SHARED as a try-lock (the GDPR erasure takes that key exclusively; it is its OWN key, so a recipient's account data, cross-signing or key backup writer that holds `sync_global|<user>` never makes a message skip), the transaction has a short `lock_timeout`, a conflict is a 429; only the recipients that passed the shared-room join are locked (never one lock per requested id); the eviction, the acknowledgement and the stamp take `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` (a row another request holds is skipped, never waited for), and a delivery error (a lock, a statement timeout) never fails `/sync`: the answer is 200 with `to_device: {"events": []}`, the position is kept (nothing acknowledged or lost), the log names the kind only; a send takes `FOR KEY SHARE` on the target `devices` rows only around its INSERT (the soft eviction runs afterwards in a second, separate transaction without any lock on `devices`; a failed eviction never fails the send and the queues catch up with the next send) and a device delete locks the device row first, so a send racing a device delete never leaves rows for a recreated device of the same id; a send is a constant number of set-based statements however many recipients it names.
 
 *Lifecycle.* A device removal (`DELETE /devices`, `/delete_devices`, logout, logout-all, the Admin UI revoke, the OAuth logout) deletes the queue and the idempotency records of the device (`DeviceKeyStore.delete_device`, one place); revoking only the sessions of a device (the device and its keys stay) does not purge its queue, an admin deactivation keeps it (reversible), the messages a removed device SENT stay in the recipients' queues. The GDPR erasure deletes the queue and records of the user and the messages the user sent. The TTL job `Nebu.Workers.ToDevicePurgeWorker` is an Oban cron job (the leader enqueues, the purge is batched atomic SQL with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` that concurrent runs split between them), no node-local state.
 
