the row first) and is then answered 429, the client retries with the same txn id (nothing is stored, the id is not burnt); the same wait also hits a send to a device whose OAuth token rotates at that moment (up to 100 ms, then 429): a weak "device active now" timing signal, only for joined co-members; (12) the REVERSE direction of that race (availability only): a send holds `FOR KEY SHARE` on the target `devices` rows until its transaction commits (about a dozen statements, among them the 7 eviction statements, up to 2 s each), and PostgreSQL grants further compatible key-share lockers without queuing them behind a waiting `FOR UPDATE`, so overlapping sends from joined co-members can starve the victim's OAuth token rotation, `/oauth2/revoke`, `DELETE /devices` and the admin revoke past their 300 ms `lock_timeout` for as long as the flood lasts; attacker cost: one joined co-member account, bounded by the per-user rate limit (60 per minute, burst 30), 4 send slots per user and 6 per node, and the byte-cost bucket; follow-up idea: take the key share right before the INSERT and run the soft eviction in a separate short transaction after the commit; (13) the per-sender total (4000 messages / 16 MiB) is a SOFT cap: it is "16 MiB + the in-flight fan-out" (concurrent sends, 4 per user and N nodes, up to 1 MiB each, and rows another transaction holds and `SKIP LOCKED` skips can leave the sender above the cap until its next send; about 4N MiB + the locked rows), no cross-user effect; (14) follow-up: a hot 200 loop after a failed or empty-while-pending delivery (while a row is pending the long poll has timeout 0 and the answer is an empty `to_device` at once, so a client re-syncs without backoff; bounded by the sync rate tier and the 2 s statement timeout, no data loss, no oracle): a node-local per-device cooldown of 1-2 s, or let the long poll wait min(timeout, 2 s); (15) `DeviceKeyStore.delete_device/2` is all-or-nothing and silent (the caller sees `:ok` even when the transaction failed; its only caller is the own device after UIA, the TTL job cleans a leftover queue): accepted. | Low | Accepted |
